## Architectural Paradigms: From Polling to Reactive Streams

In modern high-concurrency systems, traditional short-polling and even long-polling HTTP mechanisms introduce unsustainable CPU overhead and TCP handshake thrashing. When coordinating real-time events across tens of thousands of active client sessions, architecting a **bidirectional event streaming pipeline** becomes essential.

> [!NOTE]
> WebSockets provide a full-duplex communication channel over a single long-lived TCP connection, reducing protocol overhead per packet from ~800 bytes (HTTP header overhead) to just 2-6 bytes per frame.

By leveraging **Redis Streams** alongside persistent WebSocket gateways, we decouple real-time client ingestion from backend business processing workers.

---

## System Topology & Microservice Architecture

The overall cluster topology relies on horizontal gateway pods orchestrated behind an NGINX reverse proxy with sticky session IP hashing for initial WebSocket handshakes.

![Event-Driven Distributed Cluster Architecture](/assets/images/mickanic-cover.png "Real-Time Distributed Event Stream Architecture with Redis Pub/Sub & WebSockets")

### Message Flow Lifecycle

1. **Client Connection**: The client initiates an upgraded HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 WebSocket connection to the ingress gateway.
2. **Authentication & Session Attachment**: Gateway validates JWT credentials in memory and binds the socket to an ephemeral channel.
3. **Stream Production**: Inbound client actions publish directly to Redis Stream key `stream:events` via `XADD`.
4. **Consumer Worker Processing**: Dedicated Node.js backend workers read batch streams using consumer groups (`XREADGROUP`), process business rules, and acknowledge receipt (`XACK`).
5. **Targeted Broadcast**: Broadcast dispatcher publishes the computed payload to the target Redis Pub/Sub channel, which relays it directly to the connected socket instance.

